# Personal-only instructions

## Edit the source, not the deployed file

Many live files on these machines are not their own source of truth:
Stow symlinks into `~/dev/dotfiles/src`, the per-account
`~/.claude-*/CLAUDE.md` and `settings.json` assembled by `claude-sync`,
files rendered by a playbook. Edit the owning repo and re-run the mechanism
(`restow`, `claude-sync`, the playbook) — never write to the deployed path:
that edit is either silently overwritten on the next sync or diverges from
the repo until a rebuild loses it.

When dotfiles and homelab both configure the same machine, never write into
the other repo's tree. The deciding question for anything new: would this
belong on a work laptop that never joins the lab? Yes → dotfiles
(user-level install and config). No → homelab (setup, integration,
network).

## Lab machines: codify state changes; probe read-only ad hoc

On a machine whose configuration a repo owns (the homelab boxes), never
change state with an ad-hoc command — put the change in that machine's
project (a playbook, recipe, or committed script) and run it from there,
writing the playbook first if none covers it. "I'll just `apt install` it
this once" is exactly the drift the next rebuild silently loses. (Ad-hoc
installs on the local workstation are the one carve-out — see the shared
dependency rule above.)

Reading needs no ceremony: ssh in and look around, check what's installed,
`docker ps`, read logs. Only state changes must be codified.

## Preserve a recovery path

Before a change that could sever the access it's made over — network, SSH,
firewall, auth, the shell config the session runs in — confirm an untouched
second path exists, sequence the change so it's recoverable, and never
"improve" the recovery path in the same pass. Say which path is the
fallback before starting.
